  "image_name": "6c8c2d5cd996d0223cc3b1d8415f0295.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the main functional roles and interactions of major soil microbial groups. Fungi, bacteria, archaea, protists, and viruses are shown as linked parts of a soil food web and biogeochemical system. Fungi are divided into mycorrhizal forms, which move carbon from roots into soil and help plants acquire nutrients, saprotrophs that decompose litter and soil organic matter, and plant pathogens that reduce vegetation performance. Bacteria are presented as highly versatile, contributing to organic matter decomposition, nitrogen fixation, nitrification, denitrification, methylotrophy, support of mycorrhizal establishment, and nutrient release through mineral weathering. Archaea are associated mainly with methanogenesis and denitrification.\n\nThe central interactions emphasize biomass turnover and trophic control. Fungi and bacteria both participate in decomposition of dead biomass, with arrows indicating exchange through breakdown and recycling of organic material. Protists are shown as predators of fungi and bacteria, implying top-down control on these microbial populations, and some protists also perform photosynthesis. Viruses interact mainly through infection and lysis, affecting bacteria, archaea, and apparently fungi or protists, thereby returning cellular material to the surrounding system. Overall, the image highlights how different microbial groups jointly regulate decomposition, nutrient cycling, plant performance, and microbial mortality in soils.",
